<section class="panel" aria-label="Place-value addition flexible strategies">
    <h2>Warm-up — Number Talk</h2>
    <p>Mentally evaluate each in turn, then explain your preferred grouping. The teacher displays one expression at a time and compares strategies.</p>
    <ol><li>200 + 40 + 7</li><li>50 + 300 + 2</li><li>40 + 600 + 12</li><li>500 + 17 + 130</li></ol>
    <h2>Activity 1 — Strategies to Add</h2>
    <p>Use base-ten blocks, an expanded sum, a number line or a mental strategy. Compare your reasoning with a partner. If choosing three of four problems for accessibility, try to include the final two.</p>
    <ol><li>325 + 102</li><li>301 + 52</li><li>276 + 118</li><li>298 + 305</li></ol>
    <p>Discuss whether different diagrams and equations show the same place-value idea. Your teacher checks the representation, not just the result.</p>
    <h2>Activity 2 — Two Ways to Add</h2>
    <p>Andre and Clare each solve 276 + 118, but record the same partial sums in different orders.</p>
    <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(240px,1fr));gap:12px">
    <div style="border:1px solid #7d93a8;padding:12px;border-radius:8px">
      <h3>Andre — hundreds first</h3><p>200 + 100 = 300</p><p>70 + 10 = 80</p><p>6 + 8 = 14</p><p>300 + 80 + 14 = 394</p>
    </div>
    <div style="border:1px solid #7d93a8;padding:12px;border-radius:8px">
      <h3>Clare — ones first</h3><p>6 + 8 = 14</p><p>70 + 10 = 80</p><p>200 + 100 = 300</p><p>14 + 80 + 300 = 394</p>
    </div></div>
    <p>Explain what is the same and what changes; are both methods valid? Partner discussion and the MLR6 reading routine require teacher review.</p>
    <h2>Synthesis</h2>
    <p>Which representation do you like best? Does your strategy depend on the numbers? Base-ten blocks are available with the teacher. Optional centers are Target Numbers stage 6, Five in a Row stage 8 and Rectangle Rumble stage 2; original gameboards are not copied.</p>
